REQUIRES: aarch64, x86
RUN: split-file %s %t.dir && cd %t.dir

RUN: llvm-mc -filetype=obj -triple=arm64ec-windows chain.s -o chain.obj
RUN: llvm-mc -filetype=obj -triple=arm64ec-windows ref.s -o ref.obj
RUN: llvm-mc -filetype=obj -triple=arm64ec-windows %S/Inputs/loadconfig-arm64ec.s -o loadconfig-arm64ec.obj

A weak default may land on an ARM64EC symbol that is itself an anti-dependency
alias of its own mangled form. The two names denote one function, so the
fallback can continue through the pair and reach the definition.

RUN: lld-link -machine:arm64ec -dll -noentry -nodefaultlib -out:out.dll \
RUN:          ref.obj chain.obj loadconfig-arm64ec.obj
RUN: llvm-objdump -d out.dll | FileCheck --check-prefix=DISASM %s
DISASM:      0000000180001000 <.text>:
DISASM-NEXT: 180001000: d65f03c0     ret

Anti-dependency links that are not such a pair are still not chained, so this
one stays unresolved.

RUN: llvm-mc -filetype=obj -triple=arm64ec-windows unrelated.s -o unrelated.obj
RUN: not lld-link -machine:arm64ec -dll -noentry -nodefaultlib -out:bad.dll \
RUN:              ref.obj unrelated.obj loadconfig-arm64ec.obj 2>&1 | FileCheck --check-prefix=ERR %s
ERR: error: undefined symbol: vecdtor

#--- chain.s
    .text
    .globl "#target"
"#target":
    ret

    .weak_anti_dep target
.set target, "#target"

    .weak vecdtor
.set vecdtor, target

#--- unrelated.s
    .text
    .globl "#other"
"#other":
    ret

    .weak_anti_dep middle
.set middle, "#other"

    .weak vecdtor
.set vecdtor, middle

#--- ref.s
    .section .test, "r"
    .rva vecdtor
